<!--
  A second voice for /docs/concepts/, kept beside it for review. Same facts
  and the same verified Lead sample (concepts.meta.js); whichever reads
  better replaces the other, and this file goes.
-->
<script>
  import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.mesa'

  export let data = null

  const sample = data?.samples ?? {}
  const toc = [
    { href: '#why', title: 'Why one file' },
    { href: '#lead', title: 'A lead, start to finish' },
    { href: '#code', title: 'Where your code goes' },
    { href: '#more', title: 'When you need more' },
    { href: '#access', title: 'Who gets to see what' },
    { href: '#change', title: 'When something changes' },
    { href: '#surfaces', title: 'One app, many places' },
    { href: '#remember', title: 'If you remember three things' },
  ]
</script>

<DocsShell nav={data?.nav} current="/docs/concepts/" {toc}>
<article id="top" class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs · Concepts · Draft</p>
  <h1>One file, and everything that grows from it.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Read this before anything else. Once it clicks, most of FrontierJS is
    something you can guess rather than look up.
  </p>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why one file</h2>
    <p>
      Every app states the same facts several times: a column, a type, a
      validator, a form. An agent will write all of them in seconds, so typing
      isn't the problem. The copies drifting apart is, and so is having to read
      every one to find the wrong one. In FrontierJS a fact lives once, in
      <code>db/schema.lite</code>, and the rest of the app takes it from there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lead">
    <h2>A lead, start to finish</h2>
    <p>Say you're tracking sales leads. This is the whole definition:</p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.LEAD}</pre>
    <p>
      The fields read the way you'd guess. The last lines say who can do what
      (<code>@@gate</code>, <code>@@allow</code>) and how status may move
      (<code>@@transitions</code>): there's no path from <code>new</code> straight
      to <code>won</code>, because nobody wrote one.
    </p>
    <p>
      From those lines come the table and its migrations, validation on server and
      browser, access checks, types, a JSON Schema, a default form and the tools an
      AI agent sees. The schema holds what's true, never what to do, which keeps it
      short enough to read closely. <a href="/tour/derived/">Derived</a> shows it
      happening.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="code">
    <h2>Where your code goes</h2>
    <p>Three layers, one noun each, so you always know which file you want:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Noun</th><th>For our lead</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Data</td><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td><code>model Lead</code>, queried as <code>db.lead</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>API</td><td><strong>Service</strong></td><td><code>leads.service.ts</code>, at <code>/api/leads</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>UI</td><td><strong>Resource</strong></td><td><code>Lead.mesa</code>, exporting <code>leads</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.SERVICE}</pre>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.RESOURCE}</pre>
    <p>
      That empty service already lists, reads, creates, updates and deletes,
      refuses bad input and filters by who's asking. A file only grows when it has
      something to say the model can't. Two more nouns: a <strong>Release</strong>
      ships the app and a <strong>Suite</strong> tests it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="more">
    <h2>When you need more</h2>
    <p>Reach for the smallest tool that does the job:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Say it in the schema</strong> if it's a rule about the data.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a hook</strong> to step into one service's calls.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a plugin</strong> to give the whole app a new ability.</li>
      <li><strong>Swap a provider</strong> to change what's behind a seam, like mail.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A schema rule holds everywhere; a hook only holds for calls that pass
      through it. A data rule in a hook is the first thing to look for in a review.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="access">
    <h2>Who gets to see what</h2>
    <p>
      Access is declared in the schema and checked where data is read and
      written, so a route, a job, a script and an agent all pass the same check.
      Each caller sits on a ladder, and <code>@@gate</code> sets the rung needed to
      read, create, update and delete:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ladder">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>STRANGER</td><td>5</td><td>ADMINISTRATOR</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>VISITOR</td><td>6</td><td>OWNER</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>READER</td><td>7</td><td>SYSADMIN</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>CREATOR</td><td>8</td><td>SYSTEM, only through <code>asSystem()</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>USER</td><td>9</td><td>LOCKED, which nobody passes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      So <code>@@gate(2.4.4.5)</code>: readers look, users create and edit,
      administrators delete. <code>@@allow</code> narrows a user's list to their
      own leads, inside the query. The browser gets a copy to hide buttons, but the
      server is the lock. With no rules at all the schema acts like a plain ORM;
      after your first, an ungated model is closed, and every
      <code>asSystem()</code> is recorded.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="change">
    <h2>When something changes</h2>
    <p>
      A write is announced once, by the data, so every screen showing that lead
      updates, even when a job or script made the change. Screens point at data
      instead of keeping copies to refresh. Each handoff in a request has one
      owner too, so a wrong result has one place to look.
      <a href="/tour/seams/">The seams</a> follows one request through.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="surfaces">
    <h2>One app, many places</h2>
    <p>
      Beside <code>db/</code> sits a folder per place people reach the app:
      <code>api/</code>, <code>web/</code>, <code>site/</code>, and if you need them
      <code>widgets/</code>, <code>extension/</code>, <code>desktop/</code>,
      <code>mobile/</code> and <code>cli/</code>. Every interface is Mesa, built for
      its folder; code two of them share goes in <code>shared/</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remember">
    <h2>If you remember three things</h2>
    <ul class="axioms">
      <li><strong>Say it once.</strong> Every fact has one home.</li>
      <li><strong>Call it one thing.</strong> <code>Lead</code>, <code>db.lead</code>, <code>/api/leads</code> and <code>leads</code>: find one, find the others.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it one owner.</strong> Every rule and handoff lives in one place.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When FrontierJS surprises you, it's usually one of these doing its job.</p>
  </section>

  <footer class="next">
    <p>
      <strong>Next:</strong> <a href="/docs/ecosystem/">Ecosystem</a> for the
      packages around an app, <a href="/tour/">the tour</a> to see each idea run,
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/VOCABULARY.md">Vocabulary</a>
      for every defined word.
    </p>
  </footer>
</article>
</DocsShell>

<style>
  .doc { max-width: 44rem; font-family: var(--font-primary); }
  .eyebrow { font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.9rem); line-height: 1.08; letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .3em 0 .5em; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  section { padding-top: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--sticky-offset) + 1rem); }
  h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
  section p, section li { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  section li + li { margin-top: .4rem; }
  .code { font-size: var(--text-xs); margin: 1.25rem 0; }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: .95rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .5rem .75rem .5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
  th { font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--ink-mute); font-weight: 600; }
  .ladder td:nth-child(odd) { color: var(--ink-mute); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; width: 2rem; }

  .axioms { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .axioms li { padding: .4rem 0; }

  .next { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; }
  .next p { line-height: 1.75; color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
